<%#
  012 — the signed-in menu, in its two treatments.

  Below the breakpoint the menu is a disclosure: the bar is the brand and a
  toggle, and the panel holds everything else. At and above it, the bar this
  header has always had.

  WHY TWO CONTAINERS RATHER THAN ONE.

  The first attempt rendered a single <details> and took it apart on wide
  screens with `display: contents` plus `::details-content { content-visibility:
  visible }`. Visually that works — the panel lays out in the bar with a real
  box, and Chrome reports the content as visible. It was still wrong.

  Content inside a closed <details> is treated as hidden by the platform no
  matter what the computed style says. The WebDriver displayedness algorithm
  states it outright: a non-summary descendant of a <details> without `open` is
  not displayed. Tooling built on that agrees, and it is a fair bet assistive
  technology does too. A desktop bar whose links are "visible" only to sighted
  users reading pixels is not a bar that satisfies FR-011, and it would have
  been a silent accessibility regression rather than a visible bug.

  So each treatment gets its own container and exactly one is ever displayed —
  `display: none` on the other, which removes it from the accessibility tree
  entirely. No control is ever announced twice, and no control is ever present
  but unreachable. Both containers are filled from shared/_site_menu_items, so
  there is still one source of truth for what the menu contains.

  The disclosure is <details> because that is the site's disclosure already
  (002/009's locker editor, 003's wish panel, 005's decline form), and because
  opening, closing, keyboard operation and expanded/collapsed announcement are
  native — the menu works with no script at all (FR-010b). Escape and
  outside-click come from site_menu_controller.js on top of that; if it never
  loads, the toggle still opens and closes the panel (FR-010b-ii).

  Do not hand-write aria-expanded, aria-controls or role on the summary. The
  element supplies them, and overriding them is how this pattern gets broken.
%>

<%# The narrow treatment. Hidden at and above the breakpoint. %>
<details class="site-menu" data-controller="site-menu">
  <%# The accessible name describes the destination, not the drawing: someone
      hearing it announced needs to know this is the way into the navigation,
      which is what FR-017 asks for. aria-label rather than visible text because
      the control is an icon — the same choice 009's pencil made. %>
  <summary class="site-menu-toggle" aria-label="<%= t(".menu") %>">
    <span class="site-menu-toggle-bars" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  </summary>

  <div class="site-menu-panel">
    <%= render "shared/site_menu_items" %>
  </div>
</details>

<%# The wide treatment. Hidden below the breakpoint. %>
<div class="site-bar">
  <%= render "shared/site_menu_items" %>
</div>
